Supreme People's Court

Opinions on Pilot Work of Recognizing and Assisting Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Bankruptcy Proceedings

Fa Fa [2021] No. 15

In order to implement the provisions of Article 95 of the Basic Law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People's Republic of China, further improve the system of judicial assistance between the Mainland and the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, promote economic integration and development, and optimize the law-based business environment, the Supreme People's Court and the Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, based on judicial practice, held consultations on the work of mutual recognition and assistance in bankruptcy proceedings between the courts of the Mainland and the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, and signed the Minutes of Meeting between the Supreme People's Court and the Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region on Mutual Recognition and Assistance in Bankruptcy Proceedings between the Courts of the Mainland and the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region. In accordance with the spirit of the Minutes of Meeting, the Supreme People's Court formulates these Opinions based on the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law of the People's Republic of China, and other relevant laws.

1. The Supreme People's Court designates the people's courts of Shanghai Municipality, Xiamen City of Fujian Province, and Shenzhen City of Guangdong Province to carry out pilot work on recognizing and assisting Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings.

2. "Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings" as used in these Opinions refer to collective liquidation proceedings conducted in accordance with the Companies (Winding Up and Miscellaneous Provisions) Ordinance and the Companies Ordinance of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, including compulsory winding-up of companies, voluntary winding-up of companies by creditors, and corporate debt restructuring procedures proposed by a liquidator or provisional liquidator and approved by the High Court of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region pursuant to section 673 of the Companies Ordinance of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region.

3. "Hong Kong administrator" as used in these Opinions includes the liquidator and provisional liquidator in Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings.

4. These Opinions apply to Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings where the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region is the location of the debtor's center of main interests.

"Center of main interests" as used in these Opinions generally refers to the debtor's place of registration. Meanwhile, the people's court shall make a determination by comprehensively considering factors such as the location of the debtor's principal place of business, principal place of operations, and principal place of assets.

At the time the Hong Kong administrator applies for recognition and assistance, the debtor's center of main interests shall have been continuously located in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region for more than six months.

5. Where the debtor's principal assets in the Mainland are located in a pilot area, the debtor has a place of business in a pilot area, or the debtor has a representative office in a pilot area, the Hong Kong administrator may apply for recognition and assistance of the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings in accordance with these Opinions.

Cross-border bankruptcy assistance cases heard under these Opinions shall be under the jurisdiction of the intermediate people's courts in the pilot areas.

If applications are filed with two or more people's courts having jurisdiction, the case shall be under the jurisdiction of the people's court that first docketed the case.

6. When applying for recognition and assistance of Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, the Hong Kong administrator shall submit the following materials:

(1) a written application;

(2) a letter from the High Court of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region requesting recognition and assistance;

(3) documents concerning the commencement of the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings and the appointment of the Hong Kong administrator;

(4) evidence that the debtor's center of main interests is located in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region; if such evidence is created outside the Mainland, notarization and certification formalities shall also be completed in accordance with Mainland laws;

(5) a copy of the judgment or ruling for which recognition and assistance is sought;

(6) a copy of the identification document of the Hong Kong administrator; if the identification document is issued outside the Mainland, notarization and certification formalities shall also be completed in accordance with Mainland laws;

(7) evidence that the debtor's principal assets in the Mainland are located in a pilot area, that the debtor has a place of business in a pilot area, or that the debtor has a representative office in a pilot area.

If any document submitted to the people's court is not in the Chinese language, a Chinese translation shall be submitted.

7. The written application shall state the following:

(1) the name and place of registration of the debtor, and the names, positions, addresses, identification document information, and means of communication of the debtor's principal responsible persons known to the Hong Kong administrator;

(2) the name, address, identification document information, and means of communication of the Hong Kong administrator;

(3) the progress and plan of the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings;

(4) the matters and grounds for applying for recognition and assistance;

(5) the known assets, place of business, representative offices, and creditors of the debtor in the Mainland;

(6) litigation and arbitration proceedings in the Mainland involving the debtor, and any preservation measures and enforcement proceedings concerning the debtor's assets;

(7) information regarding bankruptcy proceedings against the debtor in other countries or regions;

(8) other matters that should be stated.

8. The people's court shall, within five days from the date of receiving the application for recognition and assistance, notify known creditors and other interested parties and issue a public announcement. If an interested party has an objection, it shall submit it in writing to the people's court within seven days from the date of receiving the notification or the date of the public announcement. If the people's court deems it necessary, it may hold a hearing.

9. If, after the people's court receives the application for recognition and assistance but before it renders a ruling, the Hong Kong administrator applies for preservation measures, the people's court shall handle the matter in accordance with the relevant laws and regulations of the Mainland.

10. If the people's court rules to recognize the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, it shall, upon application, simultaneously rule to recognize the identity of the Hong Kong administrator and issue a public announcement within five days.

11. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, any payment made by the debtor to an individual creditor shall be null and void.

12. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, any civil litigation or arbitration proceedings concerning the debtor that have already commenced but have not yet concluded shall be suspended; after the Hong Kong administrator takes over the debtor's assets, such litigation or arbitration shall resume.

13. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, any preservation measures concerning the debtor's assets shall be dissolved, and any enforcement proceedings shall be suspended.

14. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, it may, upon application, rule to permit the Hong Kong administrator to perform the following duties within the Mainland:

(1) take over the debtor's assets, seals, account books, documents, and other materials;

(2) investigate the debtor's financial condition and prepare a report on the financial condition;

(3) decide on the debtor's internal management affairs;

(4) decide on the debtor's daily expenses and other necessary expenses;

(5) before the first meeting of creditors is convened, decide whether to continue or cease the debtor's business;

(6) manage and dispose of the debtor's assets;

(7) represent the debtor in litigation, arbitration, or other legal proceedings;

(8) accept and examine the claims filed by Mainland creditors;

(9) other duties that the people's court considers permissible for the Hong Kong administrator to perform.

When performing the duties prescribed in the preceding paragraph, if the Hong Kong administrator intends to waive property rights and interests, create security interests over property, borrow money, transfer property out of the Mainland, or take other actions concerning property disposal that have a significant impact on the interests of creditors, separate approval from the people's court is required.

The Hong Kong administrator shall not perform duties beyond the scope prescribed by the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law of the People's Republic of China or beyond the scope prescribed by the laws of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region.

15. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, it may, upon application by the Hong Kong administrator or a creditor, appoint a Mainland administrator. After the Mainland administrator is appointed, the duties prescribed in Article 14 of these Opinions shall be exercised by the Mainland administrator, and the debtor's affairs and assets in the Mainland shall be handled in accordance with the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law of the People's Republic of China. The administrators of the two places shall strengthen communication and cooperation.

16. After the people's court recognizes the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, it may, upon application, rule to provide assistance in matters such as the realization of bankruptcy property, distribution of bankruptcy property, debt restructuring arrangements, and termination of bankruptcy proceedings. The people's court shall issue a public announcement within five days from the date of receiving the above application. If an interested party has an objection, it shall submit it in writing to the people's court within seven days from the date of the public announcement. If the people's court deems it necessary, it may hold a hearing.

17. If circumstances affecting the recognition and assistance of Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings are discovered, the people's court may modify or terminate the recognition and assistance. If the circumstances in the preceding paragraph occur, the administrator shall promptly report to the people's court and submit relevant materials.

18. If an interested party provides evidence proving any of the following circumstances, the people's court shall, after examination and verification, rule to refuse recognition or assistance of the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings:

(1) the debtor's center of main interests is not located in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, or has not been continuously located in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region for six months;

(2) the conditions set forth in Article 2 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law of the People's Republic of China are not met;

(3) Mainland creditors would be treated unfairly;

(4) there is fraud;

(5) other circumstances under which the people's court considers that recognition or assistance should be refused.

If the people's court considers that recognition or assistance of the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings would violate the basic principles of Mainland law or be contrary to public order and good morals, it shall refuse recognition or assistance.

19. If the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region and the Mainland separately conduct bankruptcy proceedings concerning the same debtor or debtors with related relationships, the administrators of the two places shall strengthen communication and cooperation.

20. If the people's court recognizes and assists Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, after the debtor's bankruptcy assets in the Mainland have satisfied the debts that should be paid preferentially in the Mainland in accordance with Mainland laws, the remaining assets shall be distributed and satisfied in accordance with the Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings, provided that creditors of the same class receive equal treatment.

21. After the people's court renders a ruling, the administrator or an interested party may, within ten days from the date of service of the ruling, apply for reconsideration to the people's court at the next higher level. Enforcement shall not be suspended during the reconsideration period.

22. An applicant for recognition and assistance of Hong Kong bankruptcy proceedings shall pay fees in accordance with the laws and regulations on litigation fees in the Mainland.

23. The pilot courts shall, in the process of hearing cross-border bankruptcy assistance cases, promptly report to or seek instructions from the Supreme People's Court on major matters.

24. The pilot courts shall actively communicate and cooperate with the courts of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region.

May 11, 2021
